<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- Fettler: the file, search and edit operations, the two front ends
       over them, and nothing else.

       R1.2 is the load-bearing decision in this file, and it is now
       stated rather than visible as an absence.

       NO ProjectReference, EVER. That is the part carrying the reason:
       Fettler shares this repository, its release and its version number
       with what else lives here, and shares nothing else.

       A PackageReference only from the allowlist below, each entry
       meeting ALL of: a permissive licence (MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD) and
       never copyleft; pure managed with no native assets, because fettle
       publishes self-contained and single-file per OS; a minimal
       transitive closure; and actively maintained. Fettler.Tests asserts
       the resulting reference set from outside (R1.4), because a
       constraint that is not tested lasts until somebody is in a hurry.

       The allowlist, in full:

         PdfPig (UglyToad.PdfPig) - Apache-2.0, pure managed C#, ZERO
         transitive dependencies, ships lib/ only and no runtimes/.
         Verified at the version pinned below rather than remembered.
         It is here because `Read` is denied to the assistant outright,
         which makes Fettler the only reader - and a developer holding a
         PDF of the requirements would otherwise simply be stranded.
         Apache-2.0 requires the licence and any NOTICE to travel with a
         distributed binary, and MIT requires this project's own copyright
         notice in every copy, so each fettle archive carries BOTH
         NOTICE and LICENSE from the top of this repository.

         The dependency stops here. PdfPig is bundled into fettle and
         into nothing else this repository distributes: burler is the
         only other program, it has no PDF component and no dependency
         on one, direct or transitive, and the no-ProjectReference rule
         above is what guarantees that. NOTICE and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md
         both say so, and ci.yml fails the build if NOTICE stops saying
         it.

       Inside this one assembly the layering of R3.1 is kept by namespace
       and asserted by test: Fettler.Core may not name System.Text.Json,
       System.Console or an exit code, because a core that knows about a
       protocol has already stopped being one. The two front ends live
       beside it in Fettler.Cli and Fettler.Mcp and are ordinary classes,
       so the tests drive both without a process. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>Fettler</RootNamespace>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="PdfPig" Version="0.1.15" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
